<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diretório dos Índios</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1757">1757</a>–<a href="/years/1798">1798</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Directorate of the Indians, Indian Directorate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Diretório dos Índios was a Portuguese law of 1757, drawn up by Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-xavier-de-mendonca-furtado">Francisco Xavier de Mendonça Furtado</a> and extended to all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> in 1758, that ended missionary control over the indigenous villages of Amazonia. The villages became Portuguese-style towns under lay directors, who organised indigenous labour, promoted marriage with settlers and imposed Portuguese in place of the língua geral. It was abolished in 1798.</p></div>
